## 2. Inspection (cite path:line for every finding)

- [ ] Names state meaning and unit; no abbreviations a newcomer must guess.
- [ ] Constants, units and literal exceptions follow the project's conventions.
- [ ] Public API and docstring documentation follows the project's policy and
      matches changed arguments, returns and errors.
- [ ] Types are concrete; no `Any`, casts or untyped public signatures.
- [ ] Code sits in the owning layer; dependencies point inward; I/O at edges.
- [ ] Errors are specific, chained and actionable; nothing is swallowed.
- [ ] Failure paths and boundary values have tests, not only the happy path.
- [ ] Documentation, examples and changelog match the new behavior.
- [ ] No secrets, personal data, absolute local paths or debug leftovers.
- [ ] No generated file was edited by hand.
- [ ] Risk-relevant callers, defaults, permissions and unchanged consumers checked.
- [ ] Each candidate has a concrete failure scenario and contrary evidence checked.
- [ ] Duplicate symptoms merged; introduced and pre-existing issues distinguished.
- [ ] Passing checks used only for the contracts they actually test.
- [ ] Revised patches resolve the original finding without new regressions.

